#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064093 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "15473603-5bb5-5a20-8fc9-e4d7589ccb38" :question/math-academy-id "e-473" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\\left(\\cot x + \\sin^2 x\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064094 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" :question/math-academy-id "e-474" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064095 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "b34db891-0676-505c-9ba3-57d86f1d9b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-475" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2621a51d-6ea2-5c0c-b0e8-ef9a5309fa09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247760} {:answer/id #uuid "679c8c73-db45-58a5-8311-c18d1aa796b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248353} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bb94e3-7ab2-5067-a9f7-907364396e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248924} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6ee47a-69b2-58b2-804c-4404e96412f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249242} {:answer/id #uuid "e05fabc1-bec3-5016-a5ff-3dafd2caba5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679c8c73-db45-58a5-8311-c18d1aa796b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248353} :answer-field/id #uuid "580bbc55-c5cf-5ded-aa0f-fb666956d730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6eaf6d0-3cca-5bcd-87e4-c38716de9570" :question/math-academy-id "q-114313" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c4c17b5-6041-5b4a-921e-16d5b13a2a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c4c17b5-6041-5b4a-921e-16d5b13a2a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319573} :answer-field/id #uuid "786408a1-4911-5c78-8041-0f51f924f24b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00fe6411-9f3d-58f9-a531-7305d81e2e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-352797" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c6f0ff-ec1f-50e3-a92a-82bffe674add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cot x - \\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063199} {:answer/id #uuid "e9193028-f868-5369-a089-f4f11d49e29d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\tan x - \\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063200} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7dd6ce-0ef2-5fcb-9d1e-f7f79d410f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cot x + \\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063201} {:answer/id #uuid "039b9216-b6cd-5595-a9cc-cfc17032ab82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan x + \\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063202} {:answer/id #uuid "1773a1d4-bddd-5a7c-8f8c-74618207142a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot x - \\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063203}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dd13680e-c437-5191-a0b8-f472cae34b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063198}] :question/id #uuid "4246a553-248d-558d-9e27-dac3cd88b439" :question/math-academy-id "q-49271" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{1}{\\cos^{2} x}(\\cot^{2} x + \\cos^{2} x\\sin x)dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc5237a-c69b-55ae-b866-010d3f7a5bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330371} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd1cdcd-2921-5ddc-b909-e7e993685c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330489} {:answer/id #uuid "50fef89f-04e0-57c1-8ae4-fc0b3a91c400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330683} {:answer/id #uuid "8245d36a-a259-5c47-8ea5-6c5ffebba5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331100} {:answer/id #uuid "912e4360-a6f3-5690-971b-e8aa27cc95d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8245d36a-a259-5c47-8ea5-6c5ffebba5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331100} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e525583-0340-50af-b1e3-515ea118e8bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c28e3047-44ec-5cae-a521-e1a19c0409df" :question/math-academy-id "q-49274" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
